{"term":"mujer caída","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"fallen_woman","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":50,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A 'fallen woman'; a woman who has lost her sexual honor or moral standing through premarital sex, adultery, or other sexual transgression against the norms of the honor/shame economy.","english_term":"fallen woman","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Implies permanent social stigma and exclusion from respectable society; the 'fall' metaphor positions sexual transgression as a moral descent with no easy recovery."}
{"term":"mujer pública","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"prostitute_euphemism","concept":"prostitute","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":28,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'public woman'; a euphemism for a prostitute, framing sex work as availability to the general public rather than naming the commercial transaction directly.","english_term":"public woman","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Now dated; historically used in legal and journalistic registers to avoid 'prostituta'; the 'public' framing contrasts with the 'private' woman (wife, mother) of the domestic sphere."}
{"term":"buena fama","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"female_sexual_honor","concept":"female_sexual_honor","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The social reputation of sexual virtue and chastity, especially applied to unmarried women; the positive pole of the female honor/shame reputation axis that communities actively managed and policed.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"No English equivalent concept: English lacks a single term for a woman's standing on the female sexual-honor continuum. The positive counterpart to mala_fama; central to marriage-market value, family alliances, and the social regulation of female sexuality across the Spanish-speaking world."}
{"term":"mala fama","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"female_sexual_dishonor","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":40,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Bad sexual reputation; the social stigma attached to a woman known or believed to be sexually promiscuous or unchaste, resulting in diminished marriage prospects and community exclusion.","english_term":"bad reputation","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"English 'bad reputation' lacks the sexual-honor-economy specificity; mala_fama indexes female sexual transgression as a collective social verdict with concrete social consequences rather than merely a personal moral failing."}
{"term":"fácil","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexually_permissive_pejorative","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'easy'; applied to a person, overwhelmingly a woman, to mean sexually available or quickly persuaded to have sex with minimal resistance or courtship.","english_term":"easy","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Mirrors English 'easy' exactly in meaning and register; applying it to a man carries far weaker pejorative force, reflecting the double standard embedded in the honor/shame economy."}
{"term":"suelta","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexually_permissive_pejorative","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":50,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'loose' or 'unbound'; applied to a woman to imply sexual looseness, lack of restraint, and willingness to engage in casual sex outside appropriate relational structures.","english_term":"loose","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Semantic structure mirrors English 'loose woman' exactly; the metaphor of constraint vs. freedom maps directly onto the honor/shame economy's expectation that women's sexuality must be bound by marriage and family control."}
{"term":"ligera","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexually_permissive_pejorative","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'light'; applied to a woman to describe sexual frivolity or looseness, implying she gives herself with little moral weight or seriousness attached to the act.","english_term":"loose","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Softer than 'suelta'; frequently appears in the fuller phrase 'ligera de cascos' (literally 'light-hoofed', meaning frivolous); the lightness metaphor implies sexual frivolity rather than the full weight of moral violation."}
{"term":"deshonra familiar","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"family_sexual_dishonor","concept":"family_sexual_dishonor","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The collective dishonor brought upon an entire family by a member's — typically a daughter's or wife's — sexual transgression outside marriage, understood as a shared social wound affecting all members' standing.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"No precise English equivalent: honor-based collective family shame for individual sexual conduct is not a named concept in Anglophone sexual vocabulary. Central to honor-killing discourse and to the social mechanisms that enforce female sexual control across the Spanish-speaking and broader Mediterranean world."}
{"term":"honor familiar","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"family_sexual_honor","concept":"family_sexual_honor","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The collective sexual honor of a family, understood as a shared social asset upheld primarily through the sexual purity and chastity of female members and the virility and cuckold-resistance of male members.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"English 'family honor' exists but lacks the specific mechanism linking a daughter's virginity or a wife's fidelity to the entire family's social standing. This positive pole is managed through chaperonage, arranged marriage, and punishment of sexual transgression."}
{"term":"puta vergüenza","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"intensified_shame_exclamation","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fucking shame'; a crude intensified exclamation of social disgrace in which 'puta' functions as a vulgar intensifier (equivalent to 'fucking') rather than literally meaning 'whore.'","english_term":"fucking disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Puta' as intensifier is a grammaticalized function distinct from its literal sex-work meaning (compare 'puta madre' as intensifier); the phrase expresses communal condemnation of sexual or moral transgression and is the oral weapon of honor enforcement."}
{"term":"desvergonzada","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"shameless_woman","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'shameless woman'; a woman who openly flouts sexual and social norms without displaying the expected modesty or remorse, placing the absence of shame itself as the primary offense.","english_term":"shameless","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The feminine grammatical form (-a) targets women specifically; in honor culture, shamelessness is a graver condemnation than the transgression itself because it forecloses the possibility of redemption through remorse and reform."}
{"term":"mujer de vida alegre","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"prostitute_euphemism","concept":"prostitute","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'woman of happy life'; a genteel euphemism for a prostitute or sexually permissive woman, softening moral condemnation with an ironic positive gloss.","english_term":"woman of easy virtue","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Sardonic undertone: 'alegre' (merry/happy) inverts the social reality of stigma; structurally parallel to French 'fille de joie' and English 'woman of easy virtue'; more literary in register than 'mujer pública.'"}
{"term":"mujer de la calle","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"street_prostitute","concept":"street_prostitution","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'woman of the street'; a sex worker who solicits clients in public spaces, specifically implying street-based rather than indoor or agency-mediated sex work.","english_term":"streetwalker","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Parallel to English 'streetwalker' and Italian 'donna di strada'; the 'street' framing also carries secondary moral homelessness — a woman expelled from the domestic sphere of respectability into the unprotected public world."}
{"term":"macho","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"dominant_masculine_identity","concept":"male_virility","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man who embodies dominant aggressive masculine virtues: physical toughness, sexual prowess, emotional stoicism, and the authority to protect and control his family's honor.","english_term":"macho","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Borrowed directly into English with the same core meaning; in Spanish, can be used straightforwardly admiringly by traditionalists; in English often carries a pejorative or ironic register that is less consistently present in Spanish usage."}
{"term":"machismo","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"male_dominance_ideology","concept":"male_virility","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The cultural ideology and practice of male dominance, sexual conquest valorization, emotional hardness, and expected female subordination embedded in traditional Spanish-speaking societies.","english_term":"machismo","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Borrowed into English as a loanword with essentially the same meaning; maps imperfectly to any single registry concept — male_virility is the closest anchor, but machismo is properly an ideological system governing both sexual and non-sexual masculine behavior."}
{"term":"machista","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"male_chauvinist","concept":"male_virility","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person, typically a man, who enacts or endorses machismo: male chauvinism, the sexual double standard, and the expectation of female sexual submission and domestic subservience.","english_term":"male chauvinist","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Functions as both noun and adjective; 'mujer machista' (machista woman who enforces gender hierarchy on other women) exists; English 'male chauvinist' is approximate but lacks the specifically sexual-honor dimension of the Spanish concept."}
{"term":"hombría","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"manliness","concept":"male_virility","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":10,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Manliness; the aggregate of masculine virtues including courage, sexual potency, decisiveness, and the capacity to defend and uphold one's own and one's family's honor against all challenges.","english_term":"manliness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Carries stronger cultural freight than English 'manliness'; specifically includes not being cuckolded and fulfilling sexual expectations of dominance; loss of hombría is a social catastrophe in honor-culture contexts."}
{"term":"virilidad","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"virility","concept":"male_virility","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Virility; the quality of being sexually potent, physically vigorous, and capable of fulfilling the reproductive and social expectations of dominant masculinity.","english_term":"virility","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Direct cognate with English 'virility'; slightly more clinical in register than 'hombría' and more narrowly focused on sexual-reproductive potency rather than the full honor-culture masculine package."}
{"term":"ser muy hombre","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"manliness_affirmation","concept":"male_virility","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'to be very much a man'; an idiomatic affirmation that someone fully embodies masculine virtues, especially sexual confidence, courage, and honor-protective authority.","english_term":"to be a real man","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Used admiringly by traditionalists and sarcastically by critics of machismo; curiously, 'ser muy hombre' can also be applied to a woman to compliment exceptional courage, importing masculine virtue into female bravery."}
{"term":"calzonazos","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"partner_dominated_man","concept":"partner_controlled_male","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'big underpants/trousers'; a man completely dominated by his wife or partner, who yields all domestic and sexual authority despite nominally being the one who wears the trousers.","english_term":"henpecked husband","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Augmentative of 'calzón' (underpants/trousers); the garment metaphor ironically inverts the 'who wears the trousers' idiom — he has oversized pants but no real authority in them; implies failure to maintain the masculine sexual and social dominance that hombría requires."}
{"term":"mandilón","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"partner_dominated_man","concept":"partner_controlled_male","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":"MX","definition":"From 'mandil' (apron); a man dominated or controlled by his wife, evoking the feminizing image of a man wearing a kitchen apron instead of exercising masculine authority outside the domestic sphere.","english_term":"henpecked husband","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Most common in Mexican and Central American Spanish; the apron metaphor specifically feminizes the man by placing him in the domestic sphere assigned to women; carries stronger feminization implication than 'calzonazos' and a sharper insult to his hombría."}
{"term":"consentidor","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"complicit_cuckold","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":48,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'one who consents or permits'; a man who knowingly tolerates his partner's infidelity rather than defending his masculine honor through confrontation, punishment, or departure.","english_term":"cuckold","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Subtler and more damning than 'cornudo' in some readings — cornudo may be deceived, but consentidor knows and allows it; in honor-economy terms, active toleration of infidelity is the greater loss of hombría because it cannot be explained by ignorance."}
{"term":"cornudo","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckold","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'horned one'; the standard Spanish term for a man whose partner is sexually unfaithful to him, derived from the pan-Mediterranean folklore of the deceived husband growing horns as a mark of his shame.","english_term":"cuckold","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Horn symbolism for cuckoldry is pan-Mediterranean and pan-European (cf. Italian 'cornuto', French 'cocu'); the 'cuernos' (horns) hand gesture directed at a man is a public act of humiliation; loss of cornudo status requires honor-restoring action in traditional communities."}
{"term":"cabrón","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"cuckold_insult","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'he-goat'; etymologically a cuckold, now the most common all-purpose Spanish expletive (bastard, fucker, asshole), having expanded far beyond its sexual origin while retaining the cuckold sense in explicitly sexual contexts.","english_term":"bastard","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Semantic evolution mirrors English 'bastard' (literal legal meaning → general insult); retains the specific cuckold meaning in contexts where infidelity is the subject; also widely used as warm address between male friends in the same register as British English 'bastard' — context determines whether it's an insult, a descriptor, or an endearment."}
{"term":"víbora","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"manipulative_treacherous_woman","concept":"emasculating_person","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'viper'; a treacherous, manipulative, or venomous woman, especially one who dominates or emasculates her male partner through cunning and malice rather than open confrontation.","english_term":"viper","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Viper' exists in English but lacks the specifically gendered sexual-emasculation connotation; in Spanish honor-culture context 'víbora' specifically invokes the machismo fear of female cunning undermining male authority from within the domestic space; also applied to treacherous people of any gender in general usage."}
